	<TR><TD COLSPAN="3" WIDTH="627"><FONT FACE="Arial, Helvetica"><FONT SIZE="+1"><B>Character Biography</B></FONT><BR><BR>
	<FONT SIZE="-1">I was born the son of a magician in Felwithe; my mother died of a wasting disease when I was young, and so I barely knew her.  My father raised me in his own craft, and by the time that I had reached maturity, I was eager to enter the world.<BR><BR>My first learning experiences came in the woods of the Greater Faydark, a forest bordering my home city.  There I fought the beasts of the forest and practiced my magics, learning to summon rudimentary items and small elemental beasts.  After a time I journeyed west to the Butcherblock Mountains, and eventually crossed the Ocean of Tears to visit the great continent of Antonica.  I spent a while in the gentle hills west of Freeport, but eventually felt the call of my home continent and returned to Faydwer.<BR><BR>After spending some more time in the Butcherblock Mountains and the Lesser Faydark, with the occasional trip to Crushbone (that cursed place of orcs), I crossed the Ocean of Tears once again and took up hunting in the Northern Desert of Ro.  Mummies and dervish cutthroats were slain aplenty, along with the occasional scarab, tarantula, and orc raider.  I then retreated from the scorching sands of Ro to the cooler plains of the East Commonlands.<BR><BR>After a time there, I found a druid who agreed to teleport me across the continent, to the Northern Plains of Karana.  From there I traveled to Qeynos, where I explored a bit in the sewers before journeying north to Blackburrow and Everfrost.  That area proved too cold for my tastes, however, and I returned to the plains of Karana, where I hunted for a short while.  But it was too dangerous for me, so I returned to the Desert of Ro once again, with a short stop in Befallen to see what the fuss was all about.  (The fuss was all about constant huge trains of powerful undead, apparently.)<BR><BR>I spent time in the Oasis of Marr, fighting crocodiles and orcs, before returning once again to the west of Antonica.  Things there were not to my liking -- not even a short stay in High Hold Pass, clearing the rocky ways of marauding orcs -- so I again returned to the desert.  However, the Oasis grew weak for my skills, and so I journeyed once again to the vast Plains of Karana, to find suitable hunting.<BR><BR>There I spent time, in the eastern plains, hunting crag spiders, but their poison was too often overwhelming, so I went southwest, to the southern plains, and hunted elephants and centaurs.  Thus, I entered my 20th season, and gained my surname: Earthgate, to always remind me of the countless summoned earth elementals that have fought by my side.<BR><BR>Starting in my 19th season, I hunted in the Southern Karanas, learning well its gentle rises and flats.  Lone centaurs, aviaks, elephants, and even the occasional treant were my quarry; then I began stalking the evil hermit in his hut.  Time and again he would come, and time and again I would slay him.  In this way did I reach my 24th season.<BR><BR>Thereafter I moved to the Lost Temple of Cazic-Thule, in whose hallways and courtyards I hunt lizards and alligators.  After a time, I returned to the Southern Karanas, to hunt the gnolls of Splitpaw.<BR><BR>Thus did I reach my 30th season, taking time off to explore the depths of Najena and the Lavastorm Mountains, and visiting the Temple of Solusek Ro to acquire some magical items that, though I cannot yet use them, will be very effective later on.  I then went to hunt Goblins in the mines beneath High Keep, while the guards trod overhead, unaware of the dangers that lurk beneath.<BR><BR>Soon, I felt a calling, to the south.  I traveled to the Butcherblock Mountains, and at the docks there, took the shuttles that left for the continent of Kunark.  I transferred to the Maiden Voyage, the grandest, largest ship in all the oceans... it defies description.  Suffice to say that I am honored that my brethren constructed such a vessel.<BR><BR>Upon arriving in Kunark, I explored the area around Firiona Vie for a while, before travelling west to the Frontier Mountains to hunt giants.  Many have fallen before my power.  There I stayed for a time, until my homeland yearned, and I returned to Faydwer to participate in a raid upon the evil Castle Mistmoore.<BR><BR>Later, I spent time again in South Karanas, killing Tesch Mas Gnolls by the score, and thus did I enter my 34th season.  I returned again to the Frontier Mountains, and made my way to my 35th season.  Since then, I have visited Kedge Keep, Dagnor's Cauldron, the Estate of Unrest, and spent time hunting goblins in the Ocean of Tears.<BR><BR>In the midst of that season, I joined with my friend Icelos Silvertress, a fellow mage of the Champions of Faydwer, to brave the dangers of Permafrost Keep and Solusek's Eye, questing for the fabled Robe of the Elements.  Alone in those dungeons, we fought past ice and fire goblins alike, and with the assistance of the great Halfling druid Synamon, were able to acquire the goblin skins we needed to gain our robes.<BR><BR>Thereafter I traveled to The Overthere, a vast plain on the north coast of Kunark.  Amidst its gentle rolls and flats sits a huge, jagged canyon, filled to the brim with the evil Iksar-scorpion hybrid: the scorpikis.  I spent much time at the foot of the Frontier Mountains in the Overthere, grouped and alone, killing sabretooths, Sarnaks, cockatrices, rhinos, and even sentient cacti!  My stay there was interspersed with journeys all over the world, including raids into the Estate of Unrest, Solusek's Eye, Permafrost (again) and Kedge Keep; bits of hunting done in the vast Dreadlands, Nagafen's Lair, and the Ruins of Old Guk; and even exploratory journeys into the Skyfire Mountains and the Burning Woods.<BR><BR>Thereby I reached my 44th season.  My 44th and 45th seasons were spent primarily in the bowels of Nagafen's Lair, clearing the dungeon of the kobolds that infest the place.  Hundreds were dispatched, to the delight of the gnomes that occupy the neighboring Solusek's Eye.  Even part of my 46th season was spent there, but then I moved back to Kunark, fighting the roaming monsters of the Dreadlands until I reached my 47th season.<BR><BR>Thereafter, with other members of the High Planes Ambassadors, I met and killed the froglok king in his lair deep inside the ruins of Old Guk, and then spent time with the undead frogloks, killing the lord, arch magus, and reanimated hand that infest that side of the dungeon, as well as countless lesser frogloks who stood in the way of me and my companions.<BR><BR>-------<BR><BR>I am always interested in exploring new dungeons, so if you ever want someone to join you there, let me know and I'll see what I can do.<BR><BR>(Picture above taken in the Great Divide, after I acquired my shiny new Robe of the Elements.  Okay, five levels after...)<BR><BR>I am well-trained in combat, but also enjoy quiet reflection at times.  Thus I often indulge in my dual pastimes of fishing the river on the east side of Felwithe while practicing my magics and contemplating life, and getting stone drunk at Tovanik's Venom, a popular tavern in Felwithe, though it has been some time since I have seen it.  If you're around at the right time, you might see me and my friends taking high dives off the white stone bridge above the river; when not in Felwithe, any high point overlooking water will do.  (I have found that it is possible to fish in the sewers of Freeport, though the catch there are, um... flavored a bit differently from those of the sea.)<BR><BR>I also fancy myself a bit of a linguist.  I am fluent in all known languages.  I am willing to teach any language I know to any comer -- save those who serve darkness: ogres, trolls, dark elves, and the like -- for a small fee (dependent on the language and the inconvenience).</FONT><BR><BR></FONT></TD></TR>
	
